Namely, we can find $\mathfrak q \subset \mathfrak q' \subset B$ with $a \in \mathfrak q'$ because $B$ is $a$-adically complete. Then $\mathfrak p' = A \cap \mathfrak q'$ contains $a$ but not $f$ hence is a height $1$ prime. Then $\mathfrak p = A \cap \mathfrak q$ must be strictly contained in $\mathfrak p'$ as $a \not\in \mathfrak p$. Since $\dim (A) = 2$ we see that $\mathfrak p = (0)$.